To find the highest common ancestor (HCA) of two nodes in a binary tree, I first need to understand what HCA means. Unlike the lowest common ancestor (LCA), which is the deepest node that is an ancestor of both nodes, the HCA is the highest (closest to the root) node that is an ancestor of both nodes. 

I'll start by considering the structure of a binary tree and how to traverse it. A common approach is to use a recursive method, checking each node to see if it is an ancestor of both target nodes. 

For each node, I'll check if it is one of the target nodes. If it is, then it's a potential HCA. If not, I'll recursively check its left and right subtrees. The first node that is an ancestor of both nodes encountered during this traversal will be the HCA.

